\par\par \b \uc2 Russia reserves fall for 1st time in 6 weeks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Toni Vorobyova   \par \uc2 460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2009\par 12:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, June 18 (Reuters) - Russia's gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves on Thursday posted their first weekly fall in six weeks, shrinking by $2.9 billion or 0.07 percent as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped buying dollars in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves, the world's third largest, stood at $406.6 billion on June 12 down from $409.5 billion a week earlier, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed.\par \par \uc2 The drop in reserves coincided with the Russian rouble posting its first weekly fall versus a euro-dollar basket in 2-1/2 months last Friday <RUS=MCX>. That eliminated the need for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars in a bid to stem rouble appreciation, as it had done in previous weeks.\par \par \uc2 "Most likely (the reserve drop) is due to the halt in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  ... and the outflow of funds from foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  correspondent accounts at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Vladimir Osakovsky, analyst at \uc2 Unicredit\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Such accounts were launched last autumn in order to enable commercial banks to keep euros and dollars in Russia rather than contributing to capital outflows from the country. As of June 1, commercial banks held $14 billion on such accounts.\par \par \uc2 "Now is a favourable moment for conversion. It seems there are sales of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of the orders from clients who have to pay taxes and salaries, or simply want to reduce their dollar holdings," said Tatiana Orlova, economist at ING.\par \par \uc2 The reserves are also affected by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  valuing its assets and converting the worth of euro holdings into dollars to give the overall reading. But the exact mechanics of how it does the calculations are unclear, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 If calculating valuation effects for the week from closing prices on Friday June 5, a 0.3 percent appreciation in the euro versus the dollar <EUR=> will have had a positive impact. But working it out from June 5's open results in a slight negative.\par \par \uc2 Officially, Russia keeps around 49 percent of its reserves dollars, 40 percent euros, 10 percent sterling and 1 percent yen. But its actual allocations can vary on any particular day due to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's activity in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Despite the weekly fall, reserves remained comfortably above the near 2-year low of $376.1 billion set in mid-March.\par \par \uc2 "It (the fall) is not a large amount, and comparable to the scale of the changes in reserves, so I don't think there is anything bad going on," said \uc2 Unicredit\uc2 's Osakovsky.\par \par \uc2 -- For the history of the reserves data see [ID:nMOS005460] (Writing by Toni Vorobyova; Editing by Victoria Main)\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA-RESERVES/|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090618e56i000p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw302\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4530\pichgoal600
