\par\par \b \uc2 Russia rouble on track for 6th week of gains\b0\par\par\uc2 352 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2009\par 09:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  MOSCOW, April 3 (Reuters) - The Russian rouble was on track for its sixth consecutive week of gains versus a euro-dollar basket on Friday, cheered by firm oil prices and signs that the worst of the slowdown may be over for the domestic economy.\par \par \uc2  The rouble's strength prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to enter the market with dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of its strategy to avoid excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, dealers said.\par \par \uc2  The rouble firmed as far as 38.54 versus the basket according to Reuters data <RUS=MCX>, its strongest since Jan. 28. Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the 38.55 level in small volumes.\par \par \uc2  "Everyone suspects that it (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is there. It's clear that if it wasn't there (the basket) would fall even further," said Alexei Kulakov, dealer at \uc2 Promsvyazbank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  The recovery of the rouble over the last six weeks marks a turnaround after months of controlled devaluation, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent tens of billions of dollars to ensure the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's adjustment to the worst economic outlook was gradual.\par \par \uc2  The devaluation took the rouble to a level which better reflected the lower oil prices, and subsequent a recovery in oil and in Russian stock markets have helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  This week has brought some glimmers of positive news on Russia's economy which faces its first recession in a decade this year. March Purchasing Managers' Indexes (PMIs) for the manufacturing and services sectors both rose to five month highs even though they remained firmly in contraction territory.\par \par \uc2  "Such improvement suggests that the worst period of contraction in Russian economy might indeed be over for now. This could feed into further improvement of investor sentiments towards Russian markets," \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  said in a research note.\par \par \uc2  Officials say capital outflows may have stopped last month, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose in the latest week in part thanks to dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   (Reporting by Andrei Ostroukh, writing by Toni Vorobyova)  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 RUSSIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -ROUBLE/|LANGEN|ABN|M|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090403e543000n0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
