\par\par \b \uc2 DAVOS-WRAPUP1-Russia to defend rouble, but bailouts limited\b0\par\par\uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2009\par 17:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Kremlin aide: \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will defend rouble beyond 41 level\par \par \uc2 * Will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  while rouble within trading corridor\par \par \uc2 * VTB: no rouble strengthening for at least a year\par \par \uc2 * First Deputy PM: businessmen cannot rely on state bailouts\par \par \uc2 * Sistema plans over $1 billion in Q1 acquisitions\par \par \uc2 By Guy Faulconbridge\par \par \uc2 DAVOS, Switzerland, Jan 29 (Reuters) - Russia will defend the rouble from sliding beyond the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading band, but indebted billionaires can no longer rely on state handouts to see them through the country's first recession in a decade.\par \par \uc2 Russia, after spending a third of its gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves propping up its crisis-hit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will step in again should the rouble hit the weaker limit of 41 to the euro-dollar basket, the Kremlin's senior economic aide said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to stay out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as much as possible to avoid artificial pressures and to allow supply to determine the market exchange rate within the band," Arkady Dvorkovich told Reuters at the World Economic Forum.\par \par \uc2 But asked if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would defend the rouble should it fall another 3 percent to the band limit, he replied: "Yes. And the market will see this."\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect Russia's economic slowdown to spur severe social tensions in coming months and Prime Minister Vladimir Putin's government has made fending off a 1998-style collapse of the rouble a cornerstone of its efforts in the crisis.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week used temporary demand for roubles from corporate tax deadlines to try to end 2-1/2 months of gradual devaluation, setting limits at 26 to 41 roubles to the basket.\par \par \uc2 But the rouble slide has since resumed and bankers say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to be tested.\par \par \uc2 The rouble fell 2.6 percent to a historic low on Thursday against the euro-dollar basket, sparking confrontation between markets and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as speculators pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  toward the weaker edge of the trading corridor.\par \par \uc2 "Given the current level of commodity prices ... and world macroeconomic conditions, the rouble can go to the upper level of the band," Dvorkovich said. "We are not afraid of that."\par \par \uc2 Andrei Kostin, chief executive of state-controlled bank VTB , said the rouble was unlikely to strengthen again for at least a year, although most of its devaluation was now over.\par \par \uc2 "There is no evidence why the rouble should be stronger over the next 12 months or even 34 months, but over the long term there is a chance it will come back to previous levels," Kostin said in an interview with Reuters Television.\par \par \uc2 STATE BAILOUTS\par \par \uc2 Russia's willingness to bail out indebted businessmen, who accumulated fabulous wealth during a 10-year bull run before overstretching themselves on the debt market, is also being tested, First Deputy Prime Minister Igor Shuvalov said in Davos.\par \par \uc2 "We see that many enterprises that we work with, and their shareholders, have started to feel that the state will save them no matter what," Shuvalov told reporters at the forum.\par \par \uc2 "We have nothing like this in our plans."\par \par \uc2 Some of Russia's richest men, including metals tycoon Oleg Deripaska, face difficulties because the value of collateral they put up as security for major loans has plummeted.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Dmitriev, chairman of state lender VEB, said Russian companies had so far asked his bank for $90 billion in assistance, nearly double the $50 billion of state money with which it has been entrusted to help refinance foreign loans.\par \par \uc2 The number of bids is a strong indication of the gravity of the economic crisis facing Russia, whose economy is forecast by the government to contract in 2009. Russian corporates have to pay back $115.7 billion in foreign debt and interest this year.\par \par \uc2 But at least one company plans to embark upon a spending spree. Leading services conglomerate Sistema will spend over $1 billion on acquisitions in the first quarter of 2009, said billionaire Vladimir Yevtushenkov, who controls the group.\par \par \uc2 "In this quarter we will have major acquisitions which will need billions of dollars," he said, without naming his targets. (Writing by Robin Paxton in Moscow; Editing by Erica Billingham)\par \par \uc2 DAVOS-RUSSIA/ (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090129e51t00218\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
