\par\par \b \uc2 Some Russia rouble depreciation appropriate -EBRD\b0\par\par\uc2 427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2008\par 15:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Nov 26 (Reuters) - Some weakening of the Russian rouble is justified at a time of weaker oil prices, but a sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move could be painful for companies, the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The EBRD expects Russian growth to show sharply to around 3 percent next year -- which would be its slowest since 1998, when the economy contracted -- if Brent oil average $75 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 Faced with slower growth and strong downward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has widened the rouble's trading corridor twice this month, each time allowing it to weaken by 1 percent versus a euro-dollar basket.\par \par \uc2 "Russia has suffered commodity price shocks. In a situation like this some depreciation of the rouble is appropriate," said Jeromin Zettelmeyer, director for policy studies at EBRD.\par \par \uc2 "But a large depreciation of the rouble could create problems because Russian companies have debt denominated in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The weakening of the rouble has attracted criticism from many analysts, who say the softly-softly approach leaves future policy unclear, meaning the pressure on the rouble remains and chances of the need for a big one-off move are growing.\par \par \uc2 The Russian economy has been hurt by a severe decline in commodity prices. Oil, Russia's main export, lost more than 60 per cent since its peak this summer.\par \par \uc2 The fall in oil revenues means Russia will need to use some of its reserves to plug holes in the budget, leaving less money in the pot for rouble-supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Alexei Kudrin said on Wednesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  would need to spend 1 trillion roubles ($36.56 billion) of its Reserve Fund to pay for budget plans.\par \par \uc2 Next year's budget was planned with a sizeable surplus on Russia's Urals oil blend averaging $95 a barrel. If oil averages $60, the budget would be balanced, and beyond that it would slip into deficit.\par \par \uc2 "Policy response to a $50 level is highly uncertain", Alex Lehmann, a senior economist at the EBRD said at a press-conference, adding that oil prices falling lower than that was unlikely.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Urals traded at $47 a barrel <URL-E>.\par \par \uc2 The EBRD, set up in 1991 to help former communist countries in Eastern Europe make the transition to market economies, comprises 61 countries, the European Community and the European Investment Bank among its 63 shareholders. (Reporting by Dmitry Sergeyev; Editing by Victoria Main)\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA-ECONOMY/EBRD|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081126e4bq0013l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
