\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 5-Russian exchange has worst day in 13-year history\b0\par\par\uc2 By Polina Vorobieva and Andrei Ostroukh   \par \uc2 809 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2008\par 08:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with c.bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , CDS, closing prices)\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Oct 6 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark RTS index posted the biggest daily percentage fall in its 13-year history on Monday, hurt by falling oil and metal prices, worries about the global financial sector and negative sentiment domestically.\par \par \uc2 The sharp sell-off in stocks weighed on the rouble, with dealers estimating the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold around $5 billion to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakening beyond the 30.41 mark versus a euro-dollar basket.\par \par \uc2 Russian deputy economy minister Andrei Klepach said on Monday authorities were working on new anti-crisis measures for markets on top of the $180 billion package already announced.\par \par \uc2 The dollar-denominated RTS index <.IRTS> broke below the 900 level for the first time since autumn 2005, falling as low as 865.55. It closed at 866.39 points, down 19.1 percent -- its biggest daily fall since it was launched in September 1995. The more liquid, rouble-denominated MICEX index <.MCX> closed down 18.7 percent at 752.00 points -- its worst performance since a 20.8 percent slump in October 1997.\par \par \uc2 The steepness of the fall prompted the MICEX exchange to halt trading three times, and RTS twice during the session.\par \par \uc2 "It's a collapse. It's panic, just panic. People are forced to sell, or simply want to leave and not think about anything," said Yevgeny Volkov, a trader at \uc2 MDM Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday showed a net private capital outflow of $16.7 billion in the third quarter, and analysts said the trend was continuing.\par \par \uc2 Illustrating the negative sentiment, the RTS exchange announced the introduction of December put and call options with a strike equivalent to the index level of 300 points -- around 65 percent below current levels.\par \par \uc2 Moscow's bourses sharply underperformed the MSCI emerging markets index <.MSCIEF> which fell 10 percent to two-year lows.\par \par \uc2 Russia's five-year credit default swaps widened 30 basis points to 300 basis points, their highest since 2004.\par \par \uc2 Russian stocks have lost half of their value since May, wreaking havoc on the repo money market where many had used shares as collateral for loans and are now facing margin calls.\par \par \uc2 SOUNDING THE ALARM\par \par \uc2 News on Friday that Russian billionaire Oleg Deripaska had to sell a stake in Canadian autoparts maker Magna due to the financial crisis continued to weigh on sentiment.\par \par \uc2 "If oligarchs can't cope with margin calls, that's saying something ... Many companies used money borrowed with stocks as collateral, (now) they are paying for such financing, and people are sounding the alarm," said Eduard Lebedev, trader at Kapital.\par \par \uc2 Russia's bourses have now broken below the lows seen in mid-September when share trading was halted for nearly two days and the Russian authorities unveiled a wide-ranging package of market measures including tax cuts and possible share purchases.\par \par \uc2 The announcement's impact on sentiment proved short-lived, with analysts noting that many of the measures have not yet been implemented while the global situation has deteriorated.\par \par \uc2 "Despite the prompt government response the Russian stock market remains in a very fragile position," Ivan Ivanchenko, analyst at VTB Capital, said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 "Sentiment is at best neutral, with concerns over weakening commodities intensifying. But positioning is still overweight. This divergence makes us uneasy in the near term. The likely hedge fund redemptions in October will only make things worse."\par \par \uc2 A 3.5 percent fall in oil prices <CLc1> added to the woes on Russia's commodity-heavy bourses, as did an 8 percent slump in copper prices to near two-year lows <MCU3>.\par \par \uc2 The country's second-largest oil-producer LUKOIL lost over 20 percent . The world's largest nickel miner \uc2 Norilsk Nickel\uc2  -- which is part-owned by Deripaska and on Friday posted a sharp drop in half-year profits -- fell 30 percent .\par \par \uc2 "I don't see a single reason why you would need to buy \uc2 Norilsk Nickel\uc2  right now. A little bit more and, given the current prices for nickel as well as the fall in copper prices, (it) will become a loss-making company," said Andrei Kukk, a senior trader at UralSib.\par \par \uc2 Deripaska's Basic Element holding company said it plans to keep its 25 percent in Norilsk regardless of market conditions.\par \par \uc2 Russia's largest lender \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  and another state-owned banking major VTB fell around 16 and 25 percent respectively, amid concerns about the health of the global financial sector.\par \par \uc2 The RTS hit a record low of 37.741 points on Oct. 2, 1998, according to Reuters data. (Reporting by Polina Vorobieva, Andrei Ostroukh, Polina Devitt and Denis Dyomkin, writing by Toni Vorobyova; Editing by Hans Peters)\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA-MARKETS/ (UPDATE 5)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081006e4a6000ea\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
