\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 7-Putin nominates financial regulator as PM   \b0\par\par\uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2007\par 19:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Sept 12 (Reuters) - Russian President Vladimir Putin has nominated Viktor Zubkov, head of the Federal Financial Monitoring Service, for the vacant post of prime minister, the speaker of the lower house of parliament said on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 COMMENTS   \par \par \uc2 JONATHAN EYAL, ROYAL UNITED SERVICES INSTITUTE, LONDON:   \par \par \uc2 "What Mr Zubkov is, is effectively a friend of Putin -- which confirms the little clique that runs Russia -- and another St Petersburger. But he is not presidential material.   \par \par \uc2 "It is an indication that Putin has not made up his mind whether he wants to go for a constitutional amendment and go for another term in office himself, or whether he wants to opt for one of the other two contenders."   \par \par \uc2 EARLIER COMMENTS   \par \par \uc2 ROLAND NASH, HEAD OF RESEARCH, RENAISSANCE CAPITAL:   \par \par \uc2 "The nomination of Viktor Zubkov to replace outgoing Prime Minister Mikhail Fradkov is at least in keeping with one well established Kremlin tradition -- it was entirely unexpected.   \par \par \uc2 "Nonetheless, our first reaction to this particular PM is that, while he is now among the front runners, we do not see him as the likely successor.   \par \par \uc2 "More likely, we see him as another means of cementing some stability during the period of power transfer, a Prime Minister who will serve under both the current and the next President.   \par \par \uc2 "The little we do know about Mr Zubkov suggests he is loyal to Putin, has proven successful in fighting some difficult political battles and holds a great deal of that precious Russian political commodity, information and kompromat -- in other words, somebody who can be relied upon to maintain stability in government during a potentially uncertain transition of power."   \par \par \uc2 CHRIS WEAFER, STRATEGIST, URALSIB:   \par \par \uc2 "Appointing someone with a successful background in fighting corruption can be seen as part of the Duma election campaign. We can expect to see a few high-profile arrests and prosecutions over the next few months.   \par \par \uc2 "It puts another name in the frame (on the Putin succession), but not into a leading position. It does highlight him as a strong candidate, but not the automatic choice -- it still needs to be someone popular and acceptable to the people.   \par \par \uc2 "He's in something of a probationary period. He needs now to prove he can be the people's choice. It's his job to lose.   \par \par \uc2 "This would be a de facto continuation of the Putin presidency, until Putin wants to take back the reins, and that won't be after four years, it will certainly be sooner. A safe pair of hands until Putin wants the job back."   \par \par \uc2 SERGEI MARKOV, HEAD OF INSTITUTE FOR POLITICAL STUDIES:   \par \par \uc2 "I think the candidacy of the prime minister will be a political appointment," Markov told the Vesti-24 TV news channel.   \par \par \uc2 "Sergei Ivanov is candidate No.1 and Dmitry Medevedev is candidate No.2. But there is always the possibility for something unexpected as Putin has shown before that he can surprise people.   \par \par \uc2 "Probably the (next prime minister) will become the (Kremlin's) candidate for the presidency but it could equally be that this person will work as the partner of the next president and will remain as prime minister under the new president in order to ensure continuity for Putin's policies."   \par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER KARPOV, \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  DEALER, ZENIT BANK:   \par \par \uc2 "There was some short-term impact of this news on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Someone tried to buy dollars, weakening the rouble slightly. Everyone understands that there is no crisis in the economy and this news is not related to the economic situation."   \par \par \uc2 OLEG VYUGIN, MDM BANK CHAIRMAN, FORMER HEAD OF FINANCIAL MARKETS WATCHDOG:   \par \par \uc2 "I do not think this will have a big impact on the markets. Such a scenario has been discussed. It was not clear when it will happen. Obviously it is the preparation for the election. What is interesting is who will head the new government. That is what the markets will be waiting for."   \par \par \uc2 STANISLAV PONOMARENKO, ANALYST AT ING BANK, MOSCOW:   \par \par \uc2 "If everything with the new appointments resolves itself quickly, we do not expect it to have a major impact on the economy."   \par \par \uc2 MARKET REACTION:   \par \par \uc2 Russia's benchmark RTS index <.IRTS> closed down 0.63 percent on the day. The rouble <RUB=> is flat against the dual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, used by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to guide \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 LINKS: - For all \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and data, 3000 Xtra users can click on: <RUSSIA>   \par \par \uc2 RUSSIA-ZUBKOV/INSTANT (INSTANT VIEW 7)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|G   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070912e39c001l4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
