\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Russia cbank mulls reserves diversification  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gleb Bryanski  \par \uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2007\par 16:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Wraps \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stories)  \par \par \uc2 TULA, Russia, Jan 24 (Reuters) - Russia may diversify further the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  structure of its $300 billion in gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's third largest, a senior central banker said on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Diversification will happen. It makes sense. We are discussing it," Alexei Ulyukayev, first deputy chairman of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia, told a seminar for journalists.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps its reserves in four \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- U.S. dollars, euros, sterling and yen. Their exact structure is a secret, but Chairman Sergei Ignatyev said last July they were composed of roughly 50 percent dollars and 40 percent euros.  \par \par \uc2 Ulyukayev said the bank may fully disclose the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  structure of its reserves this year. Officials have named the Canadian and Australian dollars and Swiss franc as possible candidates for diversification.  \par \par \uc2 Russia's gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, pumped up by windfall oil export revenues, grew by 74 percent last year to $303 billion. They have since dipped slightly to $301.7 billion as of Jan. 12.  \par \par \uc2 LESS \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Falling oil prices are likely to reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current account surplus, which hit a record $95.6 billion last year, reducing pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to curb excessive rouble appreciation, Ulyukayev said.  \par \par \uc2 "All other things being equal we may continue to use this instrument. It is very effective," said Ulyukayev, a liberal economist widely regarded as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top policy spokesman.  \par \par \uc2 "There are fewer reasons (than there were previously) for us to use the exchange rate mechanism to fight inflation, but we are free to use it."  \par \par \uc2 He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to move to pure inflation targeting but this cannot happen as long as it runs a managed float of the rouble using a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, composed of 60 U.S. cents and 40 euro cents, as its policy guide.  \par \par \uc2 "Inflation targeting combined with a regulated exchange rate is impossible. Targeting requires a free float," Ulyukayev said, adding the bank would be ready for a free float in three years.  \par \par \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that oil firms bring to Russia to pay salaries and taxes. The bank allowed the rouble to appreciate by 4.3 percent against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket in 2006, helping get inflation into single digits.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  targets 8 percent inflation in 2007.  \par \par \uc2 Ulyukayev said that, with Russia's current account surplus shrinking due to lower oil prices, the liquidity wave may ebb with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  occasionally only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth market movements.  \par \par \uc2 Lower liquidity levels should push up money market rates, forcing banks to turn to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for funds and giving it more leverage over the economy through interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 Ulyukayev said the bank was prepared to lower its refinancing rate, currently at 11 percent, which is mainly used as a ceiling for official interest rates, by a half point.  \par \par \uc2 The bank's key interest rate is its repo rate, now just over 6 percent, at which banks can raise funds from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  through repurchase \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 CAPITAL INFLOWS SEEN STRONG  \par \par \uc2 Even as the current account surplus narrows, capital inflows this year may exceed the $15 billion forecast in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy guidelines, Ulyukayev forecast.  \par \par \uc2 Major equity raising by power companies and banks will suck billions of dollars into Russia, keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  under pressure to keep the exchange rate low enough for Russian firms to compete with their international rivals.  \par \par \uc2 "What we can predict is the IPO activity of Russian companies -- Unified Energy System , Sberbank and VTB," Ulyukayev said.  \par \par \uc2 "What we cannot estimate is the inflow of speculative capital caused by exchange rate fluctuations as well as other (political) risks," Ulyukayev said, referring to a parliamentary election in December and presidential polls in March 2008.  \par \par \uc2 RUSSIA-CBANK/RESERVES (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070124e31o00329\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
